To use EclipseLink with JPA and make a PL/SQL call using custom types, you can use the following steps:

1. Define the custom types in the PL/SQL package as before.
2. Create Java classes that represent the custom types and implement the SQLData interface.
3. Register the Java classes with the OracleDriver using the registerSQLType() method.
4. Create an entity class that uses the custom types as attributes.
5. Use the @NamedStoredProcedureQuery annotation to define the stored procedure in the entity class.
6. Use the EntityManager to call the stored procedure.

Here is an example of how to do this:

// Step 1: Define the custom types in the PL/SQL package
create or replace package my_package as
  type my_type is object (
    id number,
    name varchar2(50)
  );
  type my_table is table of my_type;
  procedure my_proc(p_in in my_table, p_out out my_table);
end my_package;

// Step 2: Create Java classes that represent the custom types
public class MyType implements SQLData {
  public int id;
  public String name;

  public String getSQLTypeName() {
    return "MY_PACKAGE.MY_TYPE";
  }

  public void readSQL(SQLInput stream, String typeName) throws SQLException {
    id = stream.readInt();
    name = stream.readString();
  }

  public void writeSQL(SQLOutput stream) throws SQLException {
    stream.writeInt(id);
    stream.writeString(name);
  }
}

// Step 3: Register the Java classes with the OracleDriver
OracleDriver ora = new OracleDriver();
ora.registerSQLType("MY_PACKAGE.MY_TYPE", MyType.class);

// Step 4: Create an entity class that uses the custom types
@Entity
@NamedStoredProcedureQuery(
  name = "myProc",
